.SH hed\[cq]s own man pages
\f[CR]make man\f[R] generates roff man pages from the top\-level
\f[CR]readme.md\f[R] and every \f[CR]plugins/*/README.md\f[R] into
\f[CR]man/man1/\f[R]:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]readme.md\f[R] → \f[CR]hed.1\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]plugins/<name>/README.md\f[R] → \f[CR]hed\-<name>.1\f[R]
(\f[CR]hed\-tmux\f[R], \f[CR]hed\-vim_keybinds\f[R], \&...)
.PP
The build bakes that directory into the binary
(\f[CR]\-DHED_MAN_DIR\f[R], set from the \f[CR]MAN_DIR\f[R] makefile
variable), and this plugin prepends it to \f[CR]MANPATH\f[R] when
shelling out to \f[CR]man\f[R].
So once you\[cq]ve run \f[CR]make man\f[R], the editor can open its own
docs:
.IP
.EX
:man hed
:man hed\-tmux
:man hed\-man
.EE
.PP
A trailing \f[CR]:\f[R] is appended to \f[CR]MANPATH\f[R], so the system
pages stay reachable \[em] \f[CR]:man printf\f[R] still works alongside
\f[CR]:man hed\-tmux\f[R].
.PP
To put the pages on the system \f[CR]MANPATH\f[R] too (for
\f[CR]man hed\f[R] / \f[CR]apropos hed\f[R] in any shell):
.IP
.EX
make install\-man        \f[I]# \-> \[ti]/.local/share/man/man1\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Generating the pages requires \f[CR]pandoc\f[R].
See \f[CR]scripts/gen\-man.sh\f[R].
.SH How it works
The plugin shells out to \f[CR]man\f[R] with \f[CR]MANPAGER=cat\f[R] and
\f[CR]MANWIDTH\f[R] set to the current window width (clamped to
40\[en]200 columns), then pipes through \f[CR]col \-bx\f[R] to flatten
the overstrike backspaces into plain text.
Output is slurped into a fresh read\-only buffer with the \f[CR]man\f[R]
filetype.
.PP
The \f[CR]:man\f[R] fzf picker enumerates topics via
\f[CR]apropos . | sort \-u\f[R] and hands the lines to
\f[CR]picker_list\f[R] \[em] no direct fzf coupling, so it works with
whatever picker is registered.
.SH Requirements
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]man\f[R] and \f[CR]col\f[R] on \f[CR]$PATH\f[R] (standard on any
Unix)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]apropos\f[R] for the no\-argument fzf picker
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]pandoc\f[R] only if you want to (re)generate hed\[cq]s own man
pages
.PP
If \f[CR]man\f[R] has no entry for the topic, the plugin reports it on
the status line and is otherwise a no\-op.
.SH Disable
Set \f[CR]plugin_load(&plugin_man, \&...)\f[R] to \f[CR]0\f[R] in
\f[CR]src/config.h\f[R] and \f[CR]:reload\f[R].
The \f[CR]:man\f[R] command and the man\-buffer \f[CR]gd\f[R] binding go
away.
